        en: `
<strong>Shypit Waterfall</strong> is one of the best-known and most picturesque waterfalls in the Ukrainian Carpathians. It is located in Zakarpattia Oblast near the village of <strong>Pylypets</strong>, at the foot of the Borzhava mountain range, and is one of the region's popular natural attractions.

The waterfall is formed by the mountain stream <strong>Ploshanka</strong>, which descends through the Carpathian slopes and crosses layers of resistant rock. The water flows over a sequence of rocky ledges, producing an attractive multi-level cascade.

The waterfall has a total height of approximately <strong>14 metres</strong>. Rather than forming a single vertical curtain of water, Shypit descends across several steps and separate streams, giving it a particularly picturesque appearance among the rocks and vegetation.

The name <strong>Shypit</strong> is associated with the sound of the falling water. From a distance, the stream can resemble a quiet whisper that gradually becomes louder as visitors approach the cascades.

The volume of water changes considerably with the seasons. Shypit is especially powerful during <strong>spring</strong>, when snow melts in the Carpathians, and following periods of heavy rainfall. During drier weather, the flow becomes much gentler.

The waterfall is surrounded by characteristic <strong>Carpathian forest</strong>. Beeches, spruces, and other mountain vegetation create a green natural setting around the cascades, while the humid environment encourages mosses and ferns to grow across the rocks.

Shypit lies relatively close to the well-known mountain resort of <strong>Pylypets</strong>. A visit to the waterfall can therefore easily be combined with walks across the Borzhava range, mountain excursions, and visits to other natural attractions of Zakarpattia.

One of the best-known nearby peaks is <strong>Mount Hymba</strong>. Hiking routes lead towards its slopes from the Pylypets area, while higher sections of the Borzhava range provide extensive panoramas across the Carpathians.

Reaching Shypit does not require an advanced mountain expedition. A relatively accessible route leads to the waterfall, making it popular with families, organised groups, and travellers who want to experience Carpathian nature without undertaking a difficult multi-day hike.

The area becomes particularly popular during summer. Various <strong>informal festivals and youth gatherings</strong> have taken place near Shypit, contributing to the waterfall's reputation beyond Zakarpattia itself.

Winter gives the waterfall an entirely different appearance. During prolonged periods of freezing weather, parts of the cascade can freeze, producing <strong>icicles and large formations of ice</strong> across the rocks while water may continue flowing underneath.

Despite its popularity with visitors, Shypit's greatest attraction remains its natural setting. The mountain stream, rocky cascades, forest, and nearby slopes of the Borzhava range create a classic landscape of the Ukrainian Carpathians.

Shypit demonstrates that Ukraine's natural attractions are not limited to enormous national parks or high mountain summits. This relatively compact <strong>14-metre cascade</strong> has become one of the most recognisable natural landmarks of Zakarpattia.
`,
