        en: `
<strong>Synevyr Lake</strong> is the largest natural mountain lake in the Ukrainian Carpathians and one of Ukraine's best-known natural attractions. Located in Zakarpattia Oblast among the forested slopes of the Inner Gorgany Mountains, it is often called the <strong>"Sea Eye of the Carpathians"</strong>.

The lake lies at an altitude of approximately <strong>989 metres above sea level</strong>. Mountains and dense forests surround it on all sides, creating one of the most recognisable landscapes of the Ukrainian Carpathians.

Synevyr is relatively young in geological terms. The lake formed several thousand years ago following a <strong>major mountain landslide</strong> that blocked the valley of a mountain stream. Water gradually accumulated behind this natural barrier and created the lake.

Its surface area changes depending on rainfall and seasonal conditions and is generally around <strong>4–7 hectares</strong>. Maximum depth reaches approximately <strong>20–24 metres</strong>, making Synevyr remarkably deep for a relatively small mountain lake.

A small island appears near the centre of the lake and becomes particularly noticeable when water levels are lower. The combination of the dark blue water and central island inspired the popular comparison of Synevyr to a <strong>giant eye surrounded by Carpathian forest</strong>.

The lake's water is clean and cold. Synevyr is supplied primarily by <strong>mountain streams and precipitation</strong>, while the surrounding forests help maintain the area's natural hydrological conditions.

The lake forms part of <strong>Synevyr National Nature Park</strong>, established to protect the distinctive landscapes and ecosystems of Zakarpattia. The park covers tens of thousands of hectares of mountain forests, valleys, wetlands, and wildlife habitats.

Beech, spruce, and fir forests dominate much of the surrounding landscape, while numerous species of Carpathian wildlife inhabit the protected area. <strong>Trout</strong> live in the lake, although its protected status places significant restrictions on human activity.

Synevyr is also associated with the famous <strong>legend of Syn and Vyr</strong>. According to the romantic story, Syn, the daughter of a wealthy man, fell in love with a poor young man named Vyr. Her father opposed their relationship, and after Vyr's tragic death, Syn's tears supposedly formed the lake.

The names of the legendary lovers were combined to create <strong>Synevyr</strong>. Although the story is folklore rather than a scientific explanation of the place name, it has become an important part of the lake's cultural identity.

A large wooden sculptural composition representing Syn and Vyr stands near the lake. It has become a popular landmark and reminds visitors of the local legend.

Synevyr offers very different landscapes throughout the year. Dense greenery surrounds the lake in summer, autumn transforms the forests into shades of yellow and red, and snow covers the surrounding mountains during winter.

Walking routes provide access to the lake, but the surrounding area remains protected. Visitors are expected to respect national park regulations because Synevyr's greatest value lies in the preservation of its <strong>mountain ecosystem and natural landscape</strong>.

Its location almost a kilometre above sea level, clear water, Carpathian forests, surrounding mountains, and famous legend have made Synevyr one of the defining natural symbols of Zakarpattia and Ukraine.
`,
